---
/**
 * The card masonry — a three-column grid whose cards pack by measured row spans.
 *
 * ── EXTRACTED FROM `insights/index.astro`, FIRST CONSUMER IS PRESENTATIONS ───
 *
 * The first of the three components the index apparatus is being split into
 * (<FacetFilters>, <CardMasonry>, <ResultsFooter>). It is built here because the
 * Presentations index needs a masonry NOW and the alternative was a throwaway second
 * copy of the script — so the copy that gets written is the one that survives.
 *
 * INSIGHTS HAS NOT ADOPTED IT YET, and that is deliberate rather than unfinished. Its
 * grid is coupled to its filter in two ways this component does not yet answer: the
 * filter calls `relayout()` after changing which cards are hidden, and the truncation
 * rule (`html.js .masonry:not([data-hydrated]) > :nth-child(n + 25)`) belongs to the
 * reveal rather than to the grid. Both want designing with both pages in view, which is
 * the extraction change. `onRelayout` below is the provisional answer to the first.
 *
 * ── EVERY CHILD SELECTOR IS `:global()`, AND IT IS NOT OPTIONAL ──────────────
 *
 * The cards arrive through <slot />, and SLOTTED CONTENT CARRIES THE CALLER'S SCOPE,
 * not this component's. So `.card-masonry > :nth-child(3n + 1)` compiles to
 * `…[data-astro-cid-THIS] > …[data-astro-cid-THIS]`, the card carries the PAGE's cid,
 * and the rule matches nothing — silently. It compiles, it ships, and the columns
 * simply never apply.
 *
 * `:global()` is therefore wrapped around the child half of every one of these
 * selectors, anchored under `.card-masonry`, which IS this component's root and so
 * still carries the scope. That keeps the rules from leaking to other grids while
 * letting them reach the slotted cards. This was the failure CLAUDE.md names; it was
 * verified in the built CSS rather than assumed — see the handoff.
 *
 * ── THE DOM IS IN DATE ORDER, AND EVERYTHING FOLLOWS FROM THAT ───────────────
 *
 * Native CSS masonry does not ship, so three columns are built by hand. Cards are
 * emitted FLAT, in date order, into one grid; columns come from `nth-child`, and the
 * packing from `grid-auto-rows: 1px` plus a per-card `grid-row: span`. The rejected
 * alternative was a build-time partition into three column wrappers, which forces a
 * contiguous DOM slice per column and therefore a CSS `order` per card — buying visual
 * order at the cost of a focus mismatch, non-chronological screen-reader traversal, and
 * a future `h-feed` out of date order. See insights/index.astro for the full accounting
 * and the measurements across all three rungs.
 */
interface Props {
  /**
   * How many columns at the widest rung. Both index pages use 3; the prop exists
   * because the ladder is the one thing DESIGN.md says differs per index ("Card grids
   * — the breakpoint ladder differs per index"), and Home's card band is a plausible
   * third consumer at 2.
   */
  columns?: 2 | 3
  class?: string
  /* Forwarded to the root — grid placement from the page, and the scope attribute that
     lets a page style this component at all. */
  [key: string]: unknown
}

const {columns = 3, class: className, ...rest}: Props = Astro.props
---

<div class:list={['card-masonry', className]} data-card-masonry data-columns={columns} {...rest}>
  <slot />
</div>

<style>
  .card-masonry {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;

    /* Horizontal is grid, vertical is rhythm. The boards draw 24 between columns and
       32 between cards, which is exactly this pair. `row-gap` applies in the row-grid
       state; once spans arrive the gap is built into the span instead. */
    column-gap: var(--grid-gutter);
    row-gap: var(--rhythm-list);

    /*
     * REQUIRED, at every rung. In the row-grid state it stops cards stretching to their
     * row's height — a bare card stretched to a boxed card's height would reveal that
     * whole box on hover. In the span state it stops a card stretching to fill its span,
     * which is what leaves the trailing 32 as the gap.
     */
    align-items: start;
  }

  /*
   * ── THE LADDER ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * Mobile-first, reading the `--breakpoint-*` flags rather than restating 48rem and
   * 64rem, so tokens.css stays the only place those widths are written.
   *
   * COLUMNS COME FROM `nth-child`, NOT FROM A DATA ATTRIBUTE PER CARD. The cycle is
   * exactly a card's position among its siblings, which `nth-child` reads directly —
   * nothing to generate, nothing in the markup, and it cannot drift out of step with
   * source order.
   *
   * The EXPLICIT column is what keeps the columns independent once the spans arrive: a
   * card whose definite column sits left of the placement cursor pushes the cursor down
   * a row, so each column stacks on its own instead of sharing a row height.
   *
   * ── `of :not([hidden])` IS WHAT WILL MAKE A FILTER WORK ──────────────────────
   *
   * Plain `nth-child` counts every sibling, hidden ones included, so a filtered view
   * would keep whatever columns its survivors held in the full set — at worst leaving
   * all of them in one column. Measured on Insights before it was added: a 1789px
   * ragged bottom against 478 once the count is taken over the visible set alone.
   *
   * IT IS CARRIED HERE THOUGH NOTHING HIDES CARDS YET, deliberately. It is inert on an
   * unfiltered page and identical in behaviour, and it is part of the measured design
   * rather than something to remember to add back. Baseline widely available — Safari
   * 9, Chrome 111, Firefox 113.
   */
  @container style(--breakpoint-md: true) {
    .card-masonry {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }

    .card-masonry > :global(:nth-child(2n + 1 of :not([hidden]))) {
      grid-column: 1;
    }

    .card-masonry > :global(:nth-child(2n of :not([hidden]))) {
      grid-column: 2;
    }
  }

  @container style(--breakpoint-lg: true) {
    .card-masonry[data-columns='3'] {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    }

    .card-masonry[data-columns='3'] > :global(:nth-child(3n + 1 of :not([hidden]))) {
      grid-column: 1;
    }

    .card-masonry[data-columns='3'] > :global(:nth-child(3n + 2 of :not([hidden]))) {
      grid-column: 2;
    }

    .card-masonry[data-columns='3'] > :global(:nth-child(3n of :not([hidden]))) {
      grid-column: 3;
    }
  }

  /*
   * ── THE JS-ENHANCED STATE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * Added by the script once it has measured every card. 1px rows are small enough that
   * no two cards can share one, so the shared-row height that makes the base state a row
   * grid stops existing. `row-gap: 0` because the 32 is inside each span.
   *
   * `dense` was chosen on the eye and is paid for in tab order. Sparse packing
   * guarantees a card never appears above an earlier one, but lets a card be held down
   * by a taller card in a PREVIOUS column — measured on a corpus with one unusually tall
   * card, sparse left a 287.75px worst gap and 511px of total excess against dense's
   * 32.88 and zero. Dense can float a later card above an earlier one (21 of 45
   * positions differed on Insights, no card moving more than two slots); DOM order is
   * untouched, so screen-reader traversal and a future `h-feed` stay chronological.
   * Revisitable in one word — nothing structural depends on it.
   */
  .card-masonry.is-measured {
    grid-auto-rows: 1px;

    /* `row dense` rather than the equivalent bare `dense`, which is valid — the grammar
       is `[row | column] || dense` — but leaves the axis implied. */
    grid-auto-flow: row dense;
    row-gap: 0;
  }

  .card-masonry.is-measured > :global(*) {
    grid-row: span var(--card-span);
  }
</style>

<script>
  /**
   * Measure every VISIBLE card and hand it a row span. That is the whole job — the
   * columns are the style block's `nth-child` ladder at every state, so nothing here
   * writes a `grid-column`.
   *
   * ── WHY THIS CANNOT HAPPEN AT BUILD TIME ─────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * A card's height depends on how its title and description wrap, which depends on the
   * column width and the loaded font. Only the browser knows. Prediction is not good
   * enough here: with `grid-auto-rows: 1px` a span is a hard track size, so any
   * under-estimate makes a card overflow into its neighbour.
   *
   * ── AND WHY IT MEASURES BEFORE IT APPLIES ────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * Once the class is on, a card's grid area IS its span, so `offsetHeight` would return
   * the span rather than the content. Hence the remove / measure / add sequence.
   * `align-items: start` is what keeps the measured height the natural one rather than
   * the row's.
   */
  function enhance(grid: HTMLElement) {
    const cards = Array.from(grid.children) as HTMLElement[]

    function layout() {
      /* Back to the row grid so each card reports its own height. */
      grid.classList.remove('is-measured')

      /* Read from the cascade rather than hardcoded, so it follows `--rhythm-list` if
         that moves. Read while the row gap still applies — `is-measured` sets it to 0. */
      const gap = Math.round(parseFloat(getComputedStyle(grid).rowGap)) || 0

      /* Only what a filter left standing. A hidden card is `display: none`, so it holds
         no grid cell and reports no height — measuring one would write a span of just
         the gap. The COLUMNS are not this function's business: the ladder's
         `:nth-child(… of :not([hidden]))` counts the same visible set in CSS. */
      const visible = cards.filter((card) => !card.hidden)

      /*
       * `ceil` OF THE FRACTIONAL HEIGHT, not `offsetHeight`.
       *
       * `offsetHeight` is rounded to the nearest integer, so it rounds DOWN as often as
       * up — and a span one pixel short of its card is the one error this mechanism
       * cannot absorb, because with 1px rows the span is a hard track size and the card
       * overflows into its neighbour. Measured on Insights at the 2-column rung with
       * `offsetHeight`, the median gap came out at 30 rather than 32.
       *
       * `getBoundingClientRect().height` is fractional and `ceil` always errs long, so
       * gaps land in [32, 33) and overlap is impossible.
       *
       * Two passes on purpose: read every height, THEN write every span. Interleaving
       * would invalidate layout on each write and force a reflow per card.
       */
      const spans = visible.map((card) => Math.ceil(card.getBoundingClientRect().height) + gap)
      visible.forEach((card, i) => card.style.setProperty('--card-span', String(spans[i])))

      grid.classList.add('is-measured')
    }

    /* Fonts first: the metric-matched fallback and the real face wrap differently, so
       measuring before the swap would bake in spans for the wrong face. */
    document.fonts.ready.then(layout)

    /*
     * Column width changes with the rung, and wrapping changes with width, so spans go
     * stale on resize. ResizeObserver on the grid rather than a window listener: it
     * catches a rung crossing and anything else that changes the grid's box.
     *
     * ── THE WIDTH GUARD IS NOT AN OPTIMISATION, IT BREAKS A FEEDBACK LOOP ──────
     *
     * ResizeObserver watches BOTH axes, and applying spans changes the grid's HEIGHT —
     * so an unguarded callback re-triggers itself. It would settle rather than spin,
     * since the second pass computes identical spans, but it costs a second full measure
     * of every card and is exactly the shape that logs "ResizeObserver loop completed
     * with undelivered notifications". Only a WIDTH change can alter wrapping.
     *
     * rAF rather than a timeout because this is layout work that should land before a
     * paint. It does not fire in a background tab, which is correct: the deferred
     * measure runs when the tab becomes visible, so a stale span is never displayed.
     */
    let queued = 0
    let lastWidth = 0
    new ResizeObserver((entries) => {
      const width = Math.round(entries[0].contentRect.width)
      if (width === lastWidth) return
      lastWidth = width
      cancelAnimationFrame(queued)
      queued = requestAnimationFrame(layout)
    }).observe(grid)

    /*
     * ── THE FILTER HOOK, AND IT IS PROVISIONAL ─────────────────────────────────
     *
     * On Insights `enhance()` RETURNS `layout` and the filter script calls it directly
     * after changing which cards are hidden. That cannot work across a component
     * boundary: an Astro component's <script> is its own module, so a page script holds
     * no reference to anything in here.
     *
     * A DOM event is the cheapest bridge that does not put a function on `window`, and
     * it is dispatched AT the grid, so a page with two of them addresses each one. The
     * extraction change should confirm this is the shape it wants before <FacetFilters>
     * is written against it — hence "provisional". Nothing dispatches it today.
     */
    grid.addEventListener('card-masonry:relayout', layout)
  }

  /* `querySelectorAll`, so a page may hold more than one grid. The data attribute rather
     than the class is the hook, so restyling never breaks the behaviour — and so this
     cannot collide with the `.masonry` that Insights still owns. */
  for (const grid of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-card-masonry]')) {
    enhance(grid)
  }
</script>
